{"timestamp":"2026-05-11T22:01:13.796Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"They can find it, but it is hidden in the practical sense.\n\nIf we store it in the Git common dir, it would usually live at:\n\n```text\n<repo>/.git/entire/preferences.json\n```\n\nBut in linked worktrees, `.git` is often a small pointer file, so the real location may be back in the main checkout’s Git dir. Code should resolve it via Git’s common dir, not by assuming a literal `.git/` directory in the current worktree.\n\nTradeoff:\n\n- `.git/entire/preferences.json`: shared across worktrees, not committed, but not very discoverable or user-editable.\n- `.entire/settings.local.json`: visible in the repo tree and already part of the settings model, but each worktree needs its own copy.\n\nSo yes: if “users should be able to find/edit this file easily” matters, `.git/entire/preferences.json` is worse. If “configure once per clone across worktrees” matters more, hidden Git-dir storage is the cleanest available option.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
